common name of the plant is ‘‘nigger-head.”” The flower-head has from twelve to fifteen rays, 2 inches long, rose-colored or red, drooping; recep- tacle conical; with finely tipped chaff, longer than the disk-florets; disks purplish. The root has a brownish-black color, the epidermis shrunken causing longitudinally twisted wrinkles. Over 200,000 pounds were con- sumed in 1903.
In cross-section are seen wood-wedges and medullary rays, colored dark gray or blackish; fracture shoft and rough; taste peculiar and somewhat acrid and biting, reminding one of pyrethrum; odor heavy, mousey, accompanied by a peculiar pungency. The root contains a very small percentage of alkaloid and a crystalline principle soluble in carbon disulphide (see ‘* Drug. Cite erlsoe: p. 124). Active principle contained, apparently, in an oleoresin. Allied species; Echinacea purpurea.
